### Manual request book for the API.
###
### Expected responses below assume the stack is running with the Compose
### defaults (DETECTION_MODE=hybrid, DETECTION_THRESHOLD=0.7). Set
### DETECTION_MODE=off to exercise the routes with detection out of the way.
###
### One caveat that will otherwise look like a bug: the behavioural features
### keep a 60-second sliding window per source address. Sending more than 30
### requests from here inside a minute trips RATE_ELEVATED and everything after
### that is blocked -- correctly. Check GET /health if traffic starts being
### rejected for no visible reason; it reports how many events the window holds.

### Health check, and current sliding-window occupancy
GET http://localhost:3000/health

### Register a user (expect 201)
POST http://localhost:3000/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "alice",
  "password": "correct-horse-battery-staple"
}

### Login - correct password (expect 200 + token)
POST http://localhost:3000/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "alice",
  "password": "correct-horse-battery-staple"
}

### Login - wrong password (expect 401)
###
### The same 401 is returned for an unknown username. That is deliberate: a
### distinguishable response would let an attacker enumerate valid accounts,
### which is the first half of the credential-stuffing attack under study.
POST http://localhost:3000/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "alice",
  "password": "wrong-password"
}

### Benign search (expect 200)
GET http://localhost:3000/api/search/vulnerable?q=laptop

### SQLi payload, tautology (expect 403)
###
### Blocked by SQLI_TAUTOLOGY_CONFIRMED before the handler runs. The endpoint
### behind it builds an unsanitised query by concatenation and returns it for
### inspection, but never executes it -- a realistic attack surface without a
### genuinely exploitable target.
GET http://localhost:3000/api/search/vulnerable?q=' OR '1'='1

### SQLi payload, UNION SELECT (expect 403)
GET http://localhost:3000/api/search/vulnerable?q=' UNION SELECT username, password FROM users --

### The case the rules alone do not catch (expect 403 in hybrid mode,
### 200 in rules mode)
###
### Scores 0.5 on rules, below the 0.7 threshold. The classifier carries it
### over the line, which is the case that justifies the hybrid design.
GET http://localhost:3000/api/search/vulnerable?q=admin'-- DROP TABLE orders

### Same tautology against the parameterised endpoint (expect 403)
###
### Still blocked: detection runs ahead of the route, so it does not matter
### that this handler would have been safe.
GET http://localhost:3000/api/search/secure?q=' OR '1'='1

### Protected resource without a token (expect 401)
GET http://localhost:3000/api/orders/123

### Protected resource with a token (replace TOKEN with a value from the
### login response above; expect 200)
GET http://localhost:3000/api/orders/123
Authorization: Bearer TOKEN

### Explain a decision (expect 200, with a per-rule breakdown)
###
### Only populated when DETECTION_TRACE=1. It discloses detection logic to the
### caller, so it is a diagnostic aid rather than something to leave enabled.
GET http://localhost:3000/api/search/vulnerable?q=' OR '1'='1
